.TH CVEC_SORT 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_sort \- sort a vector with the default comparison for its type
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_sort(v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro sorts the vector in place with the default comparison function
for the element type, which it gets from the csort library; the type
decides which comparator it gets.

A char* vector can hold NULL elements. The default string comparison
procedure puts NULL before every string that is not NULL, and NULL is
equal only to another NULL, so every NULL element lands at the front of
the sorted vector, with the other elements behind them in strcmp() order.

An element type with no default comparison procedure is a compile-time
error, not a run-time error. Because the type alone decides whether a
type has a default comparison procedure, cvec_sort carries a
_Static_assert, and such a call never builds. The diagnostic names the
vector and points at cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc.

These element types sort: every standard integer type in both
signednesses, every enumerated type, float, double, long double, char*,
const char*, signed char* and unsigned char*. Each of the char pointer
types sorts as a NUL-terminated string, in strcmp() order, and uint8_t*
is an unsigned char* that sorts the same way. Sort a vector of pointers
to binary buffers with cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc and a comparator
that compares the pointers. A char pointer that is itself const or
volatile, such as const char *const, sorts too, by the content of its
string. An enumerated type is compatible with one of the standard integer
types, and the macro chooses the comparison procedure of that type.

The macro rejects at compile time the types that have no default
comparison procedure: bool, a struct, a union, a pointer to anything
other than char, and a fixed-size char array such as char[64]. A vector
of an enum type is not one of them and sorts normally. Sort a rejected
element type with cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc and an explicit
comparison procedure.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v    Vector to sort
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro sorts in place with a stable mergesort algorithm, moving the
elements inside the backing array directly, with no call for each
element access.
O(n log n) time complexity, O(n) space complexity
The macro uses the default comparison for the element type.
The macro orders a NULL char* element without dereferencing it.
The code does not compile when the element type has no default
comparison procedure; the diagnostic names the vector and points at
cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc.
cvec_find is deliberately different: it accepts every element type and
falls back to byte-wise equality, because equality can come from the
bytes of an element, but an ordering cannot.
The macro calls ccol_fatal_err() when it cannot allocate the internal
temporary buffer of the sort, as every other type-inferred macro in
cvector.h that changes a vector does (cvec_push, cvec_reserve,
cvec_append_array and the others). The vector then stays completely
unsorted.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 3);
cvec_push(vec, 1);
cvec_push(vec, 2);
cvec_sort(vec);  // vec is now [1, 2, 3]
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc (3),
.BR cvec_find (3),
.BR csort_get_default_comparison_proc (3)
